					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608953928" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1444099888 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">06 Oct 2015 02:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2387045">
									

<p>The spirit in which this is written counts for a lot. This is an exploration of the artistic impulse, but more importantly, the way that the notion comes to the artist <em>demands</em> the means that he uses. In my more optimistic moods, I imagine that five, ten years down the line, fiction will evolve to the point where communities like ours will produce works on a regular basis that demand acceptance as legitimate examples of the writing art. Pieces like this, which show the reader something common and relateable, but also necessarily unique and human, are a step forward in that regard.</p>
<p>Another thing this does well is the voice in which the character speaks. Not only does the author reach for a character a little bit out of the safety zone of most writers here, but he succeeds in making the character sound believable. Dialogue and literary voice are frequent problems around the wiki, but not in this article.</p>
<p>All in all, I can buy in. Good work.</p>

							</div>

								
